/**
 * \page time_intervals Annotating Time Intervals ⏱️
 *
 * \tableofcontents
 *
 * \section time_intervals_overview Overview
 *
 * AqNWB provides the \ref AQNWB::NWB::TimeIntervals "TimeIntervals" type for recording
 * time intervals (such as experimental epochs, trials, or invalid times), where each interval
 * is defined by a `start_time` and a `stop_time`. Additional user-defined columns can be
 * appended to store any per-interval metadata.
 *
 * NWB provides several standard `TimeIntervals` tables that can be created via convenience methods on `NWBFile`:
 * - **epochs**: \ref AQNWB::NWB::NWBFile::createEpochs "NWBFile::createEpochs" for storing yime intervals marking 
 *    coarse-grained experimental phases or subdivisions of a recording session, such as baseline, task, rest, or
 *    sleep stages
 * - **trials**: \ref AQNWB::NWB::NWBFile::createTrials "NWBFile::createTrials" for storing time intervals 
 *   corresponding to repeated experimental units with consistent structure, such as individual
 *   stimulus-response-reward cycles.
 * - **invalid_times**: \ref AQNWB::NWB::NWBFile::createInvalidTimes "NWBFile::createInvalidTimes" for indicating 
 *   time intervals that should be removed from analysis.
 *
 * AqNWB supports two complementary acquisition patterns:
 *
 * | Pattern | When to use |
 * |---------|-------------|
 * | **Row-based** (`addRow` / `addRows`) | Intervals arrive one at a time during acquisition; the total count is not known in advance. |
 * | **Column-based** (write full column vectors) | All interval data are available in memory at once and can be written in a single bulk call per column. Add additional complete columns after acquisition is completed. |
 *
 * Both patterns share the same setup steps (I/O, NWBFile, table creation, and 
 * `startRecording`) and differ only in how data are written to the table.
 * The two patterns are not exclusive and hybrid approaches are possible, 
 * but require caution. In particular when using column-based append, we must 
 * ensure that all columns have been approbriately updated such that 
 * all columns have indeed the same number rows before we use `addRow` or
 * `addRows` to avoid inconsistent data. 
 *
 * \par
 * \note
 * Chunked storage is **required** for when the total number of rows is not known in advance
 * and we need to expand the columns at runtime. The `rowChunkSize` parameter of the 
 * `createDefaultDataSpecs` controls how many rows are stored per HDF5 chunk. In practice,
 * the number of rows per chunk should typically be the same for all columns of a table. 
 * The chunking behavior can also be customized via the individual `DataSpec` of the columns. 
 * Avoid creating too small chunks (e.g,. single rows) as well as too large chunks 
 * (e.g., millions of rows per chunk).
 *
 * \par
 * \note
 * `TimeIntervals` is intended for storing general annotations of time ranges. Depending on the application
 * (e.g., when intervals are generated by data acquisition or automatic data processing), it can be useful
 * to describe intervals (or instantaneous events) in time as an \ref events "EventsTable".
 *
 * \section time_intervals_setup Setup: I/O, NWBFile, and table creation
 *
 * \subsection time_intervals_io 1. Create the I/O object
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testTimeIntervalsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_timeintervals_rowbased_io_snippet
 *
 * \subsection time_intervals_nwbfile 2. Create the NWBFile
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testTimeIntervalsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_timeintervals_rowbased_nwbfile_snippet
 *
 * \subsection time_intervals_configure_table 3. Configure and create the TimeIntervals table
 *
 * Before creating the table, build a list of column specifications using
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::TimeIntervals::createDefaultDataSpecs "TimeIntervals::createDefaultDataSpecs".
 * This factory returns the default set of columns (always including the `id`, `start_time`,
 * and `stop_time` columns) and allows you to decide to include the optional `tags`
 * column and control the HDF5 chunk size used for each column.
 *
 * To add a **custom column** to the table, create a
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::VectorData::DataSpec "VectorData::DataSpec" (or a spec for any
 * other \ref AQNWB::NWB::VectorData "VectorData" subtype) and push it onto the spec
 * vector before passing it to
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::NWBFile::createTimeIntervals "NWBFile::createTimeIntervals".
 * The spec bundles the column name, dataset configuration (data type, initial size,
 * chunk size), and description in one object. Once the spec list is ready, pass it to
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::NWBFile::createTimeIntervals "NWBFile::createTimeIntervals" together
 * with the table name and description:
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testTimeIntervalsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_timeintervals_rowbased_create_table_snippet
 *
 * \note 
 * If you do not require custom columns (or want to create them later via 
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::TimeIntervals::addColumn "addColumn)", then you can bypass the 
 * creation of the table configuration and instead configure the table directly 
 * via \ref AQNWB::NWB::NWBFile::createTimeIntervals "NWBFile::createTimeIntervals"
 * or the convenience methods \ref AQNWB::NWB::NWBFile::createEpochs "NWBFile::createEpochs",
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::NWBFile::createTrials "NWBFile::createTrials", and
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::NWBFile::createInvalidTimes "NWBFile::createInvalidTimes".
 *
 * \subsection time_intervals_start 4. Start the recording
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testTimeIntervalsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_timeintervals_rowbased_start_snippet
 *
 * \note
 * When using \ref AQNWB::IO::HDF5::HDF5IO "HDF5IO", calling `startRecording` enables
 * \ref hdf5io_swmr "SWMR mode" by default.  No new datasets or groups can be added
 * after this point unless the file is closed and reopened.
 *
 * \section time_intervals_rowbased Pattern 1: Row-based acquisition
 *
 * Use this pattern when intervals are detected one at a time during an ongoing recording
 * session and the total interval count is not known in advance.
 *
 *  \subsection time_intervals_rowbased_addrow Add rows 
 *
 * Call \ref AQNWB::NWB::DynamicTable::addRow "addRow" to append a single interval, or
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::DynamicTable::addRows "addRows" to append several intervals at once.
 * Each row is expressed as a
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::DynamicTable::RowData "DynamicTable::RowData", which is an
 * `std::unordered_map<std::string, CellValue>` that maps column names to typed scalar
 * or vector values. Row IDs are auto-generated (0, 1, 2, …) when not explicitly supplied.
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testTimeIntervalsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_timeintervals_rowbased_write_snippet
 *
 * \note
 * `addRow` and `addRows` write data immediately to the underlying HDF5 datasets by
 * extending the chunked arrays one row (or batch of rows) at a time.  Call
 * `io->flush()` at any point to ensure data are moved to disk.
 *
 * \subsection time_intervals_rowbased_stop Stop the recording
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testTimeIntervalsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_timeintervals_rowbased_stop_snippet
 *
 * \section time_intervals_colbased Pattern 2: Column-based (bulk) acquisition
 *
 * Use this pattern when all interval data are collected in memory first and can be
 * written to the file in a single pass. This is common for post-acquistion or 
 * offline processing pipelines.
 *
 * \subsection time_intervals_colbased_adddata Add data to columns
 *
 * After `startRecording`, retrieve each column object via the corresponding
 * `read*()` accessor on the table, then call
 * \ref AQNWB::IO::BaseRecordingData::writeDataBlock "writeDataBlock" to write the
 * full column vector in one call.  Finally, set the row IDs with
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::DynamicTable::setRowIDs "setRowIDs".
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testTimeIntervalsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_timeintervals_colbased_create_table_snippet
 *
 * Custom columns that were not part of the original spec list can be added after
 * table creation but **before** `startRecording()` using
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::DynamicTable::addColumn "addColumn".  Create a
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::VectorData "VectorData" object, initialize it with an
 * \ref AQNWB::IO::ArrayDataSetConfig "ArrayDataSetConfig", and pass it to
 * `addColumn`:
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testTimeIntervalsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_timeintervals_colbased_addcolumn_snippet
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testTimeIntervalsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_timeintervals_colbased_start_snippet
 *
 * Write all columns — including any custom ones — after `startRecording`:
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testTimeIntervalsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_timeintervals_colbased_write_snippet
 *
 * \subsection time_intervals_colbased_stop Stop the recording
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testTimeIntervalsWorkflowExamples.cpp example_timeintervals_colbased_stop_snippet
 *
 * \section time_intervals_further_reading Further reading
 *
 * - **Reading DynamicTable.** See the \ref read_example_dynamic_table "Reading DynamicTable" section in the reading tutorial for details on how to read data from a `TimeIntervals` table.
 * - **Tips and best practices.** See the \ref events_tips "Tips and best practices" section in the Events tutorial for general advice on chunking, writing, and customizing tables, which apply equally to `TimeIntervals`.
 */
